# Godot-MCP connection. Read by the addon at editor boot via res://.env — Godot
# is launched from the GUI, which inherits no shell exports, so a file layer is
# the only way to ship this choice with the project. A process-level GODOT_MCP_*
# variable still outranks anything here.
#
# Custom = self-hosted. The addon fetches GameDev-MCP-Server into .godot/mcp-server/
# on first connect and runs it on the host below; nothing leaves this machine and
# no account is involved. The alternative, Cloud, routes the project through
# https://ai-game.dev, which is the wrong answer for these assets.
GODOT_MCP_CONNECTION_MODE=Custom

# This file is tracked, and it can be because these two lines are a decision, not
# a secret. GODOT_MCP_TOKEN is the one key that would change that — the local
# server runs with auth=none, so nothing needs it today, and the day something
# does it belongs in a process variable rather than here.

# The port is PINNED rather than derived. Left unset, the addon hashes the project
# root into a port — deterministic here, different in any other checkout, and the
# number has to be read out of the dock before an MCP client can be pointed at it.
# A typed loopback port is honoured by both the binder and the config writer from
# McpPlugin 7.3.0 on, and the .csproj pins 7.5.2, so both sides land on this number.
#
# Two URLs come off this host and they are not interchangeable:
#   plugin dials  http://localhost:8427/hub/mcp-server
#   MCP client dials  http://localhost:8427/mcp   <- what .mcp.json carries
GODOT_MCP_HOST=http://localhost:8427
